-- 49. WHICH PROTOTYPE A VALUE HAS — the half of item 31 that had no site.
--
-- THE NOTE SAID `NO SITE IN THE CORPUS` AND IT WAS ASKING THE WRONG QUESTION.
-- It read: 167 unresolved call sites, 13 of them member calls, and NOT ONE with
-- a receiver the model values as a LITERAL. True, and `may_be_lit` is not the
-- question a prototype asks. `[1, 2, 3].join(",")` needs nothing carried as a
-- value: the receiver is an `array_expression` and the KIND is the answer.
--
-- RE-MEASURED 2026-09-08, every unresolved member call with its receiver named:
--   template_literal .concat    1    the String prototype
--   array_expression .join      1    the Array prototype
--   identifier       .next      6    a generator object, `may_be_node` EMPTY
--                                    for all six - the generator protocol's
--                                    value half, which is a different item
--   new_expression / identifier 2    a class IN this program, not a prototype
--
-- So `prototype_of[flow]` landed on the two sites that were always there, and
-- `stdlib_member[audit]` turns `the residue is the standard library` from a
-- comment into a row. WHAT REMAINS IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE SENTENCE: naming
-- `Array.prototype.join` and gating it by era needs a library this model does
-- not have, which is w_env_api_surface — and that item waits on THIS one, so
-- the dependency is now half discharged rather than blocked.
work(w_prototype_of_a_value, "DONE 2026-09-08: prototype_of[flow] derives which prototype a value has, stdlib_member[audit] names the residue, and w_env_api_surface attributed it").
