-- 52 - class_expression.
work(w_class_expression, "class_expression is READ by obj_like[flow] and node_value_kind and was declared nowhere: the model had an opinion the matrix could not report").
work_order(w_class_expression, 52).
-- DONE 2026-09-08. Four cells answered: two `handled` and two waivers, and each
-- of the four is the OPPOSITE of what consistency with `class_declaration`
-- would have written for at least one of them.
--
-- THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THE ITEM ASKED. The model already named the kind
-- in `obj_like[flow]` and in `node_value_kind`, so what did that opinion
-- DERIVE? NOTHING. Measured on a probe with each form beside its control:
-- `new Declared().hold()` resolved, `new (class {})().hold()` and
-- `new (class Hoist {})().hold()` resolved to nothing, both reported as
-- unresolved `s_member_on_ident`. `member_value[flow]` named the methods
-- correctly the whole time and no receiver could reach them.
--
-- ONE RELATION WAS THE WHOLE GAP. `class_named[flow]` reads a class's `id`
-- CHILD, and a class expression's BINDING is not its `id`. All four of its
-- consumers — the two `may_be_node` arms, `class_receiver` and `super_of` —
-- ask which NAME reaches this class, so one arm fixed the constructor edge, the
-- static/instance split and the heritage clause at once.
--
-- AND A SECOND RULE ABOUT A NAME, which `calls_in` is what exposed: an
-- anonymous class expression's constructor had NO `fn_name`, so the edge was
-- derived by `resolves` and then dropped on the way to the graph. Measured on
-- this engine, because the two forms disagree — `const A = class {}` gives
-- `A.name "A"` and a frame `A`, `const W = class Hoist {}` gives `W.name
-- "Hoist"` and a frame `Hoist`.
--
-- THE CONTROL-FLOW CELL IS `handled` AND ITS DECLARATION TWIN IS WAIVED, which
-- is a disagreement rather than an oversight: `class extends mountOf() {}` calls
-- `mountOf` when the class is EVALUATED, and `calls_in` carries `top ->
-- mountOf`, so `a_no_control_transfer` is FALSE for this kind. The declaration
-- twin has the identical exposure and its row is left alone — see
-- f_a_heritage_clause_runs_and_a_class_is_waived_as_transferring_nothing.
--
-- AND THE MODULES CELL IS WAIVED WITH ITS OWN CONTROL. `export const C = class
-- {}` puts no name in `exports_name[code]` — and neither does `export const f =
-- function () {}` nor `export const g = () => 2`, while `export function h() {}`
-- beside them does. So the silence is the `export const X = <expr>` FORM and not
-- a fact about classes, which is what makes the waiver earned instead of
-- borrowed. That gap is named below rather than left to be rediscovered.
work_state(w_class_expression, done).
work_spawned(w_class_expression, f_a_class_is_reached_by_its_binding_and_a_class_expression_is_where_the_two_come_apart).
work_spawned(w_class_expression, f_an_edge_can_be_resolved_and_still_dropped_because_the_model_cannot_name_it).
work_spawned(w_class_expression, f_a_heritage_clause_runs_and_a_class_is_waived_as_transferring_nothing).

-- WHAT THIS ITEM HANDED ON, both measured and neither this item's cell:
--   * `export const X = <expr>` exports no NAME the model can see, for any
--     expression — class, function or arrow. A modules-layer question about a
--     DECLARATION FORM rather than about any kind this matrix has a cell for.
--   * a class expression that is NOT a declarator initialiser — `f(class {})`,
--     `ns.C = class {}`, `return class {}` — reaches no name at all. That is the
--     residue `node_value_kind(class_expression)` still exists for, and it is
--     why mutant m6 in test/js-class-expression.test.ts SURVIVES: the row is
--     unkillable in this corpus for want of a SITE, not for want of a rule.
-- Neither is a (kind, layer) cell this matrix has, so neither becomes a claim:
-- a claim on a cell that is shut raises `queue_stale[audit]`, and a claim on a
-- cell nobody has is not expressible. They are prose on purpose.

-- 53 - update_expression, big_int_literal, reg_exp_literal.
-- TEN OF TWELVE CLOSED 2026-09-08, and the two that are not are handed to the
-- item that can take them rather than waived under this one.
--
-- THE ITEM'S SHARPEST RESULT IS IN THE SCANNER AND NOT IN A LAYER.
-- `BigIntLiteral.value` is a native `bigint`, which matched NONE of the four
-- branches in scanners/js_ast.ts — not a node, not a node array, not a
-- string/number/boolean scalar, not a nested object — so it fell through all of
-- them and emitted nothing. `const b = 1n` reached the store as an `ast_node`
-- with ZERO `ast_attr` rows: the only literal in JavaScript whose value the
-- scanner dropped, while the contract line at the top of that file says `every
-- scalar own property`. `extra.raw` holds the text `1n` and `extra` is declared
-- out on purpose, so nothing else carried it either. Two words of TypeScript
-- and one shared `isScalar` predicate; the finding is that nothing could have
-- reported it, because a dropped attribute is indistinguishable from a node
-- that has none.
--
-- THE CONTROL-FLOW HALF WAS A DEFECT IN A NEIGHBOUR. A C-style `for` has four
-- children and `guard_kind` had ONE of them. Measured on the single C-style
-- `for` this corpus already contained (alpha.mjs:845): `guard_arm` named its
-- body, `guarded[code]` was EMPTY for the `j += 1`, so the model positively
-- asserted that a for-update runs — and `for (i = 0; i < 0; i++)` runs it zero
-- times. A CALL written there was `reached_unguarded` and therefore kept OUT of
-- `may_not_run`, which is the narrow direction this layer documents as the
-- dangerous one. The slot had been unexamined because the form that inhabits it
-- was not in the vocabulary: `i++`.
--
-- THE CALLGRAPH QUESTION THE ITEM ASKED — does a regexp literal resolve a
-- member call — HAS THE ANSWER "NO, AND IT WAS NOT EVEN NAMED". Measured before
-- any row was written: `/x[0-9]/.test(s)`, `MATCHER.test(s)` and
-- `255n.toString(16)` were all `unresolved_call` with `prototype_of[flow]`
-- EMPTY, so `stdlib_member[audit]` — the relation that exists to collect this
-- residue — could not see them, and `stdlib_member` stood at 3 rows naming only
-- a template and two arrays. Two `kind_prototype` rows and two
-- `builtin_prototype` rows put all three in it. That NAMES the residue and
-- resolves nothing, which is why the two callgraph cells are re-pointed at
-- `w_env_api_surface` below instead of being waived here: every method on a
-- regexp or a bigint is in a library this model does not have, and an `ignored`
-- would be a blocker wearing a waiver's face.
--
-- `update_expression` AT DATAFLOW IS THE ONE WAIVER THE MEASUREMENT EARNED.
-- The value is ToNumeric(operand) plus at most one, and a known literal operand
-- does not help — `"5"++` is the NUMBER 5 — which is `plain_assign`'s own
-- reason for refusing `x += y`. The WRITE half was measured rather than argued:
-- `assigns[code]` has exactly one arm, over `assignment_expression`, so `let
-- counter = 0; counter++` leaves `counter` carrying only its initialiser; and
-- across the five rule packs there are ZERO negations over a value relation
-- (`not may_be_lit`, `not may_be_node`, `not valued`, `not assigns`, `not
-- var_flow`), with a positive control of eight negations over other relations
-- in the same grep. So the missing write is an absence in a may-set and can
-- never become a positive false claim.
work(w_update_and_literals, "DONE 2026-09-08: 1n reached the store with no value at all (a bigint matched none of the scanner's four branches), a for-update was never a guard arm, and a regexp literal is the one literal whose value is an object").
work_order(w_update_and_literals, 53).
work_state(w_update_and_literals, done).
work_spawned(w_update_and_literals, f_a_bigint_matched_none_of_the_four_branches).
work_spawned(w_update_and_literals, f_a_for_update_was_never_a_guard_arm).
claim(queued, js, update_expression,           none, callgraph,    w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, update_expression,           none, dataflow,     w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, update_expression,           none, controlflow,  w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, update_expression,           none, modules,      w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, big_int_literal,             none, dataflow,     w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, big_int_literal,             none, controlflow,  w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, big_int_literal,             none, modules,      w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, reg_exp_literal,             none, dataflow,     w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, reg_exp_literal,             none, controlflow,  w_update_and_literals).
claim(queued, js, reg_exp_literal,             none, modules,      w_update_and_literals).
-- THE TWO THAT ARE NOT THIS ITEM'S. Both cells are the standard library and
