-- 54 - meta_property.
work(w_meta_property, "new.target and import.meta: one node kind and two questions").
work_order(w_meta_property, 54).
-- PARTLY DONE 2026-09-08, and the item stays OPEN with three of its four cells
-- unanswered ON PURPOSE. Closing them would need one verdict to cover two
-- constructs, and the measurement says they are two.
--
-- THE SCANNER DISTINGUISHES THEM AND NOTHING ABOVE IT DID. Measured: a
-- `meta_property` node carries ZERO `ast_attr` rows of any key and exactly two
-- identifier children, `meta` and `property` — so `new.target` is
-- (meta="new", property="target") and `import.meta` is
-- (meta="import", property="meta"). The distinction was on the store the whole
-- time and no relation read it, which is the shape this loop keeps finding.
--
-- WHAT WAS ANSWERED, AND IT IS NOT ONE OF THE FOUR LAYERS. The ERA table said
-- `kind_needs(js, meta_property, meta_property)` with `feature_since(...,
-- 2015)`, and that is WRONG FOR HALF THE KIND: `new.target` is ES2015 and
-- `import.meta` is ES2020. Neither refinement the era layer already had could
-- split it — `attr_needs` reads an attribute and there is none, and
-- `outside_attr_needs` reads an ANCESTOR while the difference is inside — so a
-- FOURTH gate table, `child_needs(Lang, Kind, Field, Name, Feature)`, was
-- written beside them. `uses_kind[audit](meta_property, F)` now returns two
-- features five years apart from one node kind, and es2015 refuses exactly the
-- later one.
--
-- ONE CELL CLOSED. `controlflow` is waived, and the reason is measured rather
-- than borrowed from the neighbours: a `meta_property` contains exactly its two
-- identifiers and neither is a `site[code]`, with a positive control that the
-- containment query does return sites for a node that has one. Both forms are
-- LEAVES — two tokens joined by a dot, and neither token is a lookup.
work_state(w_meta_property, open).
-- ...and it waits on the standard library BY NAME for half of one cell, which
-- is the dependency `unresolved_call` on `import.meta.resolve` would sit in.
work_needs(w_meta_property, w_env_api_surface).
work_spawned(w_meta_property, f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child).

-- THE THREE THAT STAY OPEN, each with the measurement that keeps it open.
--
-- callgraph: BOTH forms stand in a callee-object position and the model buckets
--   them as `o_other`. `import.meta.resolve("./x")` is a real call edge into the
--   HOST, which is w_env_api_surface's programme, and `new new.target()` is a
--   transfer site whose value is the construction mode. A waiver here would
--   claim there is no call-graph question and there are two. NOTE: no site for
--   either is in the corpus, deliberately — facts/js-shapes.rofl waives
--   `s_member_on_other` as `a_catch_all_empty_by_design` and a site would make
--   that waiver false for a reason belonging to another item.
claim(queued, js, meta_property,               none, callgraph,    w_meta_property).
-- dataflow: `may_be_node[flow]` is EMPTY for both, measured against 1 125 valued
--   expressions in the same world. `import.meta` is a host object with no node
--   in any program; `new.target` is the CONSTRUCTION MODE, and this model has no
--   relation for call mode at all — a function reached by both `f()` and
--   `new f()` has one body and one answer here. The second half is the sharper
--   one and it is this item's own.
claim(queued, js, meta_property,               none, dataflow,     w_meta_property).
-- modules: `import.meta` IS a module construct — the module's own metadata — and
--   `new.target` is not, so `a_not_a_module_construct` is true of exactly half
--   the kind. THE CELL IS KEYED BY KIND AND THE QUESTION IS NOT, and the shape
--   axis that could split it applies to `callgraph` only (facts/js-shapes.rofl).
--   Left open rather than half-waived; see
--   f_one_node_kind_two_features_and_the_era_table_could_not_look_at_a_child.
claim(queued, js, meta_property,               none, modules,      w_meta_property).

-- 55 - export_specifier, export_namespace_specifier.
work(w_export_specifier_forms, "export { a as b } and export * as ns: the two export forms with no node in this vocabulary").
work_order(w_export_specifier_forms, 55).
-- DONE 2026-09-08. All eight cells answered: seven `handled` and one waiver,
-- and the waiver is the one the measurement earned rather than the one the
-- pattern suggested.
--
-- THE ANSWER TO THE QUESTION THE ITEM ASKED. Does the model resolve
-- `export { internal as external }` to `internal` or to `external`? To NEITHER.
-- Measured on a two-file probe before a rule was written: `exports_name`'s only
-- arm reads the declaration's `declaration` child, an `export { }` list has
-- none, and the probe returned exactly one row — the ordinary `export function
-- plain` beside it — while the cross-file call `usesShown -> hidden` was absent
-- from `calls_in` with its site reported as an unresolved `s_identifier`. The
-- model was SILENT, not wrong-named. That is the safe direction.
--
-- AND AT ONE LAYER IT WAS NOT SILENT AND NOT SAFE. A module whose whole export
-- surface is a specifier list had an EMPTY entry surface — `exported_fn` walks
-- the CONTAINMENT of an export declaration and a specifier list contains two
-- identifiers and no function — so `no_entry_point[audit]` fired on the file and
-- `may_not_be_reached` named a function that file exports AND calls. A smaller
-- seed reports live functions as maybe-dead, which is the direction
-- `export_kind_unseen[audit]` already exists to catch, arriving through a FORM
-- rather than through a misspelling.
--
-- THE ONE WAIVER: `export_namespace_specifier` at CONTROLFLOW. Measured from the
-- scanner rather than inherited from `export_all_declaration`'s row — an
-- ExportNamespaceSpecifier carries an `exported` child and NO `local` child at
-- all, so there is no name in this module for the entry surface to reach.
work_state(w_export_specifier_forms, done).

-- 56 - labeled_statement.
-- DONE 2026-09-08. THE ITEM'S NOTE CALLED IT "the one new statement form that
-- really does transfer control" AND THAT WAS THE HALF WORTH MEASURING. Measured
-- first, on a fixture built because the corpus held ZERO labels: with
-- `break outer` in the inner of two nested loops, `after_abrupt[code]` named
-- `pastBreak` and `pastContinue` - the siblings in the reference's OWN
-- statement list, which an unlabelled break kills too - and named none of
-- `pastLabelledBreak`, `pastLabelledContinue`, `pastLabelledBlock`. So the
-- control-flow layer WAS TREATING A LABELLED BREAK AS AN ORDINARY ONE, and the
-- one thing a label is for was invisible.
--
-- ONE OF THE THREE WAS NOT MERELY MISSING. A labelled LOOP body is a
-- `guard_kind` arm, so `pastLabelledBreak` was in `guarded` on other grounds
-- and the may-set was already hedging. A labelled BLOCK is not a loop, not an
-- arm and not a switch: `pastLabelledBlock` was in NEITHER `guarded` NOR
-- `may_not_run`, so the model positively said a statement runs which the only
-- path through its own block skips. That is the narrow direction, which this
-- layer documents as the dangerous one.
--
-- `r_labelled_boundary` reads the LABEL and not the keyword - `label_ref` joins
-- through the `label` child, which an unlabelled break does not have - so
-- `beyondPlainBreak` stays out, and the walk stops at the `labeled_statement`,
-- so `beyondLabel` and `afterBlock` stay out too. `label_boundary` is a SIXTH
-- mechanism rather than a sixth kind filed under `abrupt`, because a label
-- receives a transfer instead of performing one.
work(w_labelled_control, "DONE 2026-09-08: r_labelled_boundary; a labelled statement, and break/continue to a label - the one new statement form that really does transfer control").
work_order(w_labelled_control, 56).
work_state(w_labelled_control, done).
work_spawned(w_labelled_control, f_a_labelled_break_was_read_as_an_ordinary_one).
claim(queued, js, labeled_statement,           none, callgraph,    w_labelled_control).
claim(queued, js, labeled_statement,           none, dataflow,     w_labelled_control).
claim(queued, js, labeled_statement,           none, controlflow,  w_labelled_control).
claim(queued, js, labeled_statement,           none, modules,      w_labelled_control).

-- 57 - empty_statement, debugger_statement, with_statement.
-- EIGHT OF TWELVE CLOSED 2026-09-08, and the four that are not are the item.
-- Their claims are the only ones left below: a closed cell whose claim stays
-- queued under an item that is not done is `queue_stale[audit]`, and the
-- convention this file already follows (see w_cf_accessor's optional twin) is
-- that the claim goes when the cell does.
--
-- THE NOTE SAID "no layer is LIKELY to have an opinion" AND THE CONTROL-FLOW
-- LAYER HAS ONE. Measured with the fixture in the corpus: of four
-- `empty_statement` nodes THREE are `guarded[code]`, TWO are a `guard_arm`
-- (`while (total < 0) ;` and an `if` consequent) and ONE is `after_abrupt`;
-- of two `debugger_statement` nodes one is guarded and one is after an abrupt
-- transfer. An inert statement OCCUPIES a conditional position; it does not
-- CREATE one, which is the claim the cell asks about, and the two are different
-- sentences. The other three layers were asked the same way and answered zero
-- with a positive control on every query - see facts/js-statements.rofl.
--
-- `with_statement` IS OPEN AND THE REASON IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ARGUED.
-- scanners/js_ast.ts calls babel with `sourceType: 'module'` and nothing else;
-- a module is strict; `with (o) { p(); }` comes back
--
--     ast_parse_error[code]("with_mod.js", "'with' in strict mode. (1:0)")
--
-- with `nodes = 0` and an EMPTY kind set. The refusal is total, so a `with`
-- anywhere in a fixture does not merely fail to produce a node - it deletes
-- that whole file from the corpus. THAT IS A SETTING AND NOT A CONSTRAINT, and
-- the distance was measured rather than assumed, which is the correction
-- f_the_contract_excluded_one_property_in_the_whole_language paid for one
-- component over: the SAME source under `sourceType: 'script'` parses and emits
-- `WithStatement`, and under `sourceType: 'unambiguous'` babel chooses `script`
-- for it while choosing `module` for ALL FIVE existing corpus files - measured
-- file by file, so the change would leave the present corpus byte-identical.
--
-- SO THE CELLS ARE WORKABLE AND ARE LEFT UNBLOCKED ON PURPOSE. What they are
-- waiting for is not a parser but a decision plus the analysis that comes with
-- it: `with` REDIRECTS NAME RESOLUTION at run time, so every identifier in its
-- body may name a property of the object instead of the binder the value layer
-- resolves it to. `sees_binder`, `may_be_node` and every call edge that rests
-- on them would be reporting a binding the language does not guarantee. That is
-- an unsoundness at the dataflow and call-graph layers, not an inert statement,
-- and it is why this is left as work rather than waived at four layers on the
-- strength of the word "inert". See f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard.
work(w_inert_statements, "empty and debugger are answered at four layers each; `with` is open because the scanner parses every file as a module - a setting, measured - and because it redirects name resolution").
work_order(w_inert_statements, 57).
work_state(w_inert_statements, open).
work_spawned(w_inert_statements, f_with_is_a_scanner_setting_and_a_scoping_hazard).
claim(queued, js, with_statement,              none, callgraph,    w_inert_statements).
claim(queued, js, with_statement,              none, dataflow,     w_inert_statements).
claim(queued, js, with_statement,              none, controlflow,  w_inert_statements).
claim(queued, js, with_statement,              none, modules,      w_inert_statements).

-- 58 - directive, directive_literal, interpreter_directive.
-- DONE 2026-09-08. Twelve cells, twelve waivers, and the item's own premise was
-- the first thing refuted: it asks whether the scanner drops directives and it
-- does not. babel hangs a directive prologue off a `directives` ARRAY and a
-- hashbang off `interpreter`, scanners/js_ast.ts walks every own property, so
-- all three kinds arrive with their values on the store. Nothing needed fixing
-- in the scanner; the question was what the model does with facts it has.
--
-- THE NEGATIVE THAT CARRIES ALL TWELVE IS ONE QUERY, not twelve. The kernel
-- reifies rule bodies, so `slot_atom(ast_node, 2, K)` is the set of node kinds
-- any rule NAMES (52 across the eight js rule packs) and `slot_atom(ast_child, 2, F)`
-- the set of fields any rule READS (34). None of `directive`,
-- `directive_literal`, `interpreter_directive` is in the first; neither
-- `directives` nor `interpreter` is in the second; `string_literal` and `body`
-- are the positive controls. Twelve empty membership queries would each have
-- been a fact about the query.
--
-- FOUR THINGS MEASURED THAT THE PLAN DID NOT EXPECT:
--   * `abrupt_kind(directive)` - the most aggressive false claim this layer can
--     make - moves NOTHING. `abrupt_at` wants a node at an index of a
--     `stmt_seq_field` (`body` or `consequent`) and a directive is in
--     `directives`, so the model cannot place one in statement order at all.
--     A survivor, and asserted as one.
--   * ...but `guard_kind(directive, value)` moves `guarded` 428 -> 431, so the
--     layer is not blind to the node and the waiver is a decision.
--   * `literal_kind(directive_literal)` DERIVES three `may_be_lit` rows. The
--     first draft of the fact pack said it derived none; the mutant refuted the
--     comment before it was committed. No answer moves - a value with no
--     consumer rather than a value the model cannot compute.
--   * AND THE ERA TABLE IS WRONG ABOUT THE HASHBANG. See the finding: `#!` is
--     the ES2023 Hashbang Grammar and `kind_baseline(js, interpreter_directive)`
--     says es5 takes it; stating the true year puts the feature in
--     `feature_unreachable[audit]` because the scale stops at ts5/2022. The row
--     stays, annotated, because both answers are false and only an `es2023` on
--     the scale fixes it - which is the owner's.
work(w_directives, "'use strict' and the hashbang: directives are a real construct and the scanner emits them").
work_order(w_directives, 58).
work_state(w_directives, done).
work_spawned(w_directives, f_the_era_scale_tops_out_below_the_hashbang_and_the_table_says_es5_takes_it).
work_spawned(w_directives, f_use_strict_changes_two_things_this_model_has_neither_of).
claim(queued, js, directive,                   none, callgraph,    w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive,                   none, dataflow,     w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive,                   none, controlflow,  w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive,                   none, modules,      w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive_literal,           none, callgraph,    w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive_literal,           none, dataflow,     w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive_literal,           none, controlflow,  w_directives).
claim(queued, js, directive_literal,           none, modules,      w_directives).
claim(queued, js, interpreter_directive,       none, callgraph,    w_directives).
claim(queued, js, interpreter_directive,       none, dataflow,     w_directives).
claim(queued, js, interpreter_directive,       none, controlflow,  w_directives).
claim(queued, js, interpreter_directive,       none, modules,      w_directives).

-- 59 - THREE KINDS THE VOCABULARY DID NOT TAKE ON, each for a MEASURED
-- reason rather than a preference, and all three wait on the same two things:
-- a parser plugin and an environment.
--   class_accessor_property   the scanner returns NO node at all for
--                             `accessor a = 1` without the decorators plugin
--   import_attribute          scannable today, but `import attributes` is
--                             ES2025 and the highest environment on the scale
--                             is ts5 at 2022, so the feature would sit in
--                             `feature_unreachable[audit]` for ever
--   export_default_specifier  `export v from` is a Babel proposal and NO
--                             environment claims it, not even ts5
-- The first two land with the decorator work, which needs the same plugin and
-- will need the same question asked of the environment scale.
work(w_plugin_gated_kinds, "class_accessor_property, import_attribute and export_default_specifier: two need a parser plugin and two need an environment the scale does not have").
work_order(w_plugin_gated_kinds, 59).
work_state(w_plugin_gated_kinds, open).


-- ==========================================================================
-- 60 - THE THREE CELLS ITEM 50 COULD NOT CLOSE, plus one it had to REOPEN.
--
-- MEASURED AT RUN TIME, seven cases, recording which functions ran: an array
-- pattern over a custom iterable runs the iterator; an object spread over an
-- object with a getter runs the getter; an array spread and a spread ARGUMENT
-- both run the iterator; an object rest runs the source`s getters. So
-- `a_no_control_transfer` is FALSE for all three, and it was already false for
-- `object_pattern`, which carried that reason from the forty-cell sweep - its
-- row is gone from facts/js-controlflow.rofl and its cell is claimed here.
--
-- THIS IS `accessor_call` WEARING ANOTHER SYNTAX. The layer already models one
-- hidden call - `transfer_mechanism(member_expression, accessor_call)` reached
-- by `accessor_read` - and every case above is the same shape: a construct
-- whose surface is a binding and whose semantics is a call. What it needs is
-- the receiver`s ITERATOR, which is a standard-library question, so this waits
-- on the same chain `w_effect_layer` does.
--
-- `assignment_pattern` IS NOT HERE and that is the discriminating half: it was
-- closable with the machinery this layer already has, because its transfer is
-- an arm skipped by a condition rather than a call the syntax hides.
work(w_destructuring_hides_a_call, "an array pattern, an object rest and every spread position transfer control into user code - an iterator or a getter - and the layer`s reason atom for all four says they transfer nothing").
work_order(w_destructuring_hides_a_call, 60).
work_state(w_destructuring_hides_a_call, open).
work_needs(w_destructuring_hides_a_call, w_env_api_surface).
claim(queued, js, object_pattern,              none, controlflow,  w_destructuring_hides_a_call).
claim(queued, js, array_pattern,               none, controlflow,  w_destructuring_hides_a_call).
claim(queued, js, rest_element,                none, controlflow,  w_destructuring_hides_a_call).
claim(queued, js, spread_element,              none, controlflow,  w_destructuring_hides_a_call).
work_spawned(w_destructuring_hides_a_call, f_destructuring_hides_a_call_the_way_an_accessor_does).

-- 2026-09-08. The last pin in test/worklist.test.ts that is still a number, and
-- the only assertion there whose SUBJECT is the size of the open set. Three
-- parallel branches moved it from 102 to 70 in one afternoon; a fourth would
-- have moved it again, and no branch can know the merged value. Whether it can
-- be stated as an identity - the open set against a relation derived some other
-- way - is a real question and not a tidy-up.
work(w_open_cell_should_be_an_identity, "the open-cell count is the last pin in the queue tests that is a number, and three parallel branches move it at once").
work_order(w_open_cell_should_be_an_identity, 61).
work_state(w_open_cell_should_be_an_identity, open).

-- ==========================================================================
-- 62 - WHAT A DECORATOR DOES TO THE THING IT DECORATES.
--
-- The CALL is modelled (r_decorator_call, 2026-09-08) and the RESULT is not.
-- A decorator returns a replacement: `@decoOnce class Decorated {}` binds
-- whatever `decoOnce` gave back, so the value layer's answer for `Decorated`
-- should be the decorator's return and today it is the class. Same for a
-- decorated method and for an auto-accessor, whose get/set pair a decorator may
-- replace wholesale.
--
-- IT IS A VALUE QUESTION AND NOT A CALL QUESTION, which is why it is a separate
-- item rather than a loose end on the one that landed: `returns[flow]` already
-- carries what a function gave back, so the join exists; what is missing is a
-- rule saying that a decorated NAME evaluates to it.
--
-- AND `class_accessor_property` IS HERE FOR A DIFFERENT REASON. Its callgraph
-- and dataflow cells are open because the corpus has a DECLARATION and no READ:
-- `accessor slot = 4` synthesises a getter and a setter, and nothing in this
-- corpus calls them. The control-flow cell is waived on the measured ground
-- that both accessors are language-created, so no transfer into USER code
-- happens - which is exactly what makes the other two cells about the value
-- layer rather than about the call graph.
work(w_decorator_replaces_its_target, "a decorator returns a replacement and the value layer still answers the original: the call is modelled, the result is not").
work_order(w_decorator_replaces_its_target, 62).
work_state(w_decorator_replaces_its_target, open).
claim(queued, js, decorator,                   none, dataflow,     w_decorator_replaces_its_target).
claim(queued, js, class_accessor_property,     none, callgraph,    w_decorator_replaces_its_target).
claim(queued, js, class_accessor_property,     none, dataflow,     w_decorator_replaces_its_target).
